-- Passage rg7-authors-purpose-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('eafa22fb-ff01-57f2-a13a-058b0e050868', 'rg7-authors-purpose-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-authors-purpose'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Keeper Nobody Named$q$, $q$Visitors to the Harrow Point lighthouse museum read a sign that names Captain Elias Brandt as the keeper of the light from 1871 to 1899. The sign is accurate, but it tells only part of the story. Records in the town archive show that his daughter, Marisol Brandt, did much of the work.

Keeping a lighthouse was hard, steady labor. Every evening, someone had to climb ninety steps, trim the wick, and fill the lamp with oil. Every morning, someone had to polish the glass so the beam would shine clearly that night. The captain's logbooks from his later years are written in a different hand than his early ones. Experts who compared the writing found that it matches letters Marisol wrote to her cousin.

In those letters, Marisol describes climbing the tower during winter storms while her father rested with an injured knee. She also mentions a night in 1888 when she kept the lamp lit for fourteen hours straight so that a fishing fleet could find the harbor.

Some local historians argue that the sign should stay as it is. They point out that the job officially belonged to the captain, and that the government paid him, not his daughter. That is true. But an official title does not show who climbed the stairs. History should record the people who did the work, not only the people whose names were on the paperwork.

The museum is planning new signs next year. Adding Marisol Brandt's name would give visitors a fuller and more honest picture of life at Harrow Point.$q$, 'draft')
