-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5cbbd877-599a-5549-811c-a3a084e856ef', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Across the West by Horse$q$, $q$Text 1: Relay (a poem)

The station keeper shouts my name.
A fresh horse stamps and steams.
I swing the mail pouch to its back
and gallop through my dreams.

The prairie blurs, the river flashes,
the dust lifts like a sigh.
Twelve miles, a fresh horse, twelve miles more,
beneath the open sky.

I never see the letters' ends,
the faces when they read.
I only know my part is speed,
and speed is all they need.

But riders whisper by the fire
of poles and humming wire.
Someday a word will cross the land
without a horse to tire.

Text 2: The Pony Express (a factual account)

The Pony Express carried mail across the American West from April 1860 to October 1861. Its route stretched nearly 2,000 miles, from St. Joseph, Missouri, to Sacramento, California.

The service worked as a relay. Stations stood roughly every 10 to 15 miles along the trail. At each station, a rider switched to a rested horse within minutes. After many stations, a new rider took over. The mail rode in a leather cover called a mochila, which slipped over the saddle. Riders were usually young and lightweight, so the horses could run faster.

Under good conditions, a letter crossed the route in about ten days. That was far quicker than a stagecoach. However, the Pony Express lasted only about eighteen months. In October 1861, workers finished a telegraph line across the country. Messages could now travel by wire in moments, and the riders were no longer needed.$q$, 'draft')
