-- Passage rg7-comparing-texts-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('425533a7-f548-511c-b3d7-b5d543092bb1', 'rg7-comparing-texts-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-comparing-texts'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Along the Towpath$q$, $q$Text 1: Mule Boy (a story)

Ezra Vance was eleven the summer he became a driver on the Erie Canal. Every morning he walked the towpath beside two mules named Clover and Duke. A long rope stretched from their harnesses to the boat floating behind them, heavy with barrels of flour.

The work was slow. The mules plodded, the boat glided, and the sun climbed. Ezra counted the bridges and sang to keep the mules moving. At the locks, he held his breath as the gates closed and the water rose, lifting the boat like a giant hand.

One evening, Captain Osei pointed toward the town ahead. "Ten years ago, that was a few cabins," he said. "Now look at it. The canal made it grow." Ezra looked at the busy docks and felt proud, as if he had built them himself.

Text 2: The Erie Canal (a factual account)

The Erie Canal opened in 1825 in the state of New York. It ran about 363 miles, connecting the Hudson River at Albany with Lake Erie at Buffalo. Before the canal, moving goods across that distance by wagon was slow and expensive.

Canal boats had no engines at first. Instead, mules or horses walked along a dirt path beside the water, called a towpath, pulling the boats with long ropes. The animals were often guided by young drivers, some of them children.

Because the land rose and fell, the canal used locks. A lock is a chamber with gates at each end. Water is let in or out to raise or lower a boat to the next level.

The canal greatly lowered the cost of shipping. Towns along its route grew quickly as trade increased.$q$, 'draft')
