-- Passage rg7-connections-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a9953528-1267-5f9f-995d-f8bac7ece0ad', 'rg7-connections-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Beaver as an Engineer$q$, $q$When a family of beavers moves into a stream, the whole valley begins to change. Beavers build dams out of sticks, mud, and stones to create a deep pond. The deep water protects their lodge from predators, and it keeps the entrance from freezing solid in winter.

The pond itself sets off a chain of other changes. Water that once rushed downhill now spreads out and slows down. Because the water moves slowly, mud and soil settle to the bottom instead of washing away. Over time, this settled soil becomes rich ground where grasses and shrubs can grow.

The slower water also soaks into the ground around the pond. During dry summers, nearby land stays green while other areas turn brown. Some ranchers have noticed that their cattle find more to eat near beaver ponds during droughts.

Many other animals benefit as well. Frogs lay eggs in the calm water. Ducks and herons hunt for food along the edges. Trees that drown in the flooded areas become homes for woodpeckers and insects.

Not everyone welcomes beavers, though. Their ponds can flood roads and farm fields, and they cut down trees that people want to keep. In some places, workers now install pipes through the dams. The pipes keep the water from rising too high while still letting the beavers stay.

A single dam may look like a messy pile of sticks. Yet by changing how water moves, a few small animals can reshape an entire landscape.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
