-- Passage rg7-context-clues-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('562d7655-9a75-5a37-a4c3-c4b0b5d8c3b5', 'rg7-context-clues-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Lights in the Deep$q$, $q$Far below the ocean's surface, where sunlight never reaches, the water is not completely dark. Many creatures there are luminous, which means they produce their own light. A deep-sea shrimp might glow blue, while a tiny jellyfish might flash green when something touches it.

Scientists call this ability bioluminescence. It happens when chemicals inside an animal's body mix together and release energy as light. Unlike a light bulb, this light gives off almost no heat.

Animals use their glow in different ways. The anglerfish has a glowing growth that dangles in front of its mouth like a fishing pole. Small fish swim toward the light, thinking it might be food. This lure brings the meal right to the anglerfish's jaws.

Other animals use light to stay hidden. Some fish have glowing patches on their bellies. From below, these patches blend with the faint light coming from above, so predators looking up cannot see the fish's outline. A fish that would otherwise be conspicuous becomes nearly invisible.

Studying these animals is difficult. Many of them are elusive, slipping away from cameras and nets so often that some species have been seen only a handful of times. Researchers now use quiet robot submarines with dim red lights, which most deep-sea animals cannot detect. In this way, scientists hope to observe the animals without scaring them off.$q$, 'draft')
