-- Passage rg7-context-clues-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('c5fa3ddc-cb94-52dd-b35e-dd1bb6aa05b6', 'rg7-context-clues-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Keepers of the Light$q$, $q$For hundreds of years, lighthouses guided ships safely along rocky coasts. Before electricity, each light needed a keeper, a person who lived at the lighthouse and made sure the lamp never went out.

The job required keepers to be vigilant. They stayed awake through long nights, watching the flame and the sea. If a storm rolled in or the lamp began to flicker, a keeper had to notice right away and act.

Many lighthouses stood near treacherous waters. Hidden rocks, strong currents, and sudden fog could tear a hole in a wooden ship within minutes. A steady light warned sailors to steer clear.

Much of a keeper's work was tedious. Every day, the keeper trimmed the lamp's wick, polished the glass lenses, and refilled the oil. The same chores repeated day after day, with little to break the routine. Keepers also kept a logbook, a daily record of the weather, passing ships, and any repairs.

In the twentieth century, automatic lights began to replace human keepers. These lights switched on by themselves at dusk and could run for months with only an occasional visit from a technician. As a result, the old job of lighthouse keeper became obsolete. Today, very few lighthouses have anyone living in them.

Some communities have turned their lighthouses into small museums. Visitors can climb the narrow spiral stairs, look out over the water, and imagine what it was like to spend a stormy night keeping the light alive.$q$, 'draft')
