-- Passage rg7-context-clues-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b5772e65-3b59-557f-87d0-d2c8c33f67f3', 'rg7-context-clues-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Wall$q$, $q$The final hill of the Riverside cross-country course had a reputation. Older runners called it the Wall, and Keanu Akana had heard the stories all season. From the bottom, the hill looked daunting, so steep and long that Keanu felt his stomach twist just looking at it.

Coach Lindqvist had given the team one piece of advice. "Set a steady pace early," she said. "Don't sprint the first mile and leave nothing for the end." Keanu had practiced running at the same speed, mile after mile, until it felt natural.

When the race started, dozens of runners shot ahead. Keanu let them go. By the second mile, he began passing the runners who had started too fast. Some were walking with their hands on their hips.

Then he reached the Wall. Halfway up, his legs began to falter. His steps grew shorter and wobblier, and for a moment he thought he might stop. He remembered his coach's words, shortened his stride, and pumped his arms.

At the top, the course flattened, and the finish line came into view. Keanu crossed it in ninth place, his best finish ever. He was exhausted, yet he felt exhilarated, as if he could have shouted loud enough to be heard across the whole park.

His teammate Dalia Mensah ran over and slapped him on the back. "You conquered the Wall," she said, grinning.$q$, 'draft')
