-- Passage rg7-context-clues-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5243e0d5-17a1-555c-a1fd-bf7701767dfa', 'rg7-context-clues-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Printing in Three Dimensions$q$, $q$Most printers put ink on flat paper. A 3D printer does something different: it builds solid objects one thin layer at a time. The printer heats a strand of plastic until it softens, then squeezes it through a nozzle onto a platform. Each new layer must adhere to the one beneath it, sticking firmly so the object does not fall apart.

Engineers often use 3D printers to make a prototype, an early sample model that lets them test an idea before making the final product. If a prototype of a new bicycle part cracks during testing, the engineer can change the design and print another one the same afternoon.

3D printers can create intricate shapes that would be hard to make any other way. A printed object might have tiny tunnels, interlocking gears, or a lacy pattern of holes, all formed in a single piece.

The machines are also versatile. In one week, a school's printer might produce a replacement knob for a cabinet, a model of a human heart for science class, and a set of chess pieces for the library.

Doctors have found ways to customize printed objects for individual patients. Instead of choosing a brace in small, medium, or large, a patient can receive one shaped to match the exact curve of an arm or leg. Because every body is a little different, a brace that fits one person perfectly may feel uncomfortable on someone else.$q$, 'draft')
