-- Passage rg7-context-clues-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b0107dcf-55f9-532f-9b4d-c14c6745b250', 'rg7-context-clues-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-context-clues'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Mural on Fifth Street$q$, $q$For years, the brick wall beside the Fifth Street laundromat had been drab. Its gray paint was peeling, and nobody gave it a second look. Then the neighborhood council announced a contest: students could submit designs for a mural to cover the wall.

Yusuf Okafor and his friend Mei Lin decided to collaborate. Yusuf was good at drawing people, and Mei was good at choosing colors, so they agreed to combine their talents on a single design.

Their first sketch was tentative. They drew faint pencil lines, erased them, and drew them again, unsure whether their idea would work. Mei kept asking, "Is this too much?" and Yusuf kept answering, "Maybe."

After a week, their confidence grew. They filled the design with neighbors they knew: the mail carrier waving, kids jumping rope, and Mrs. Alvarez watering her window boxes. Mei chose vibrant oranges, deep blues, and bright greens, colors so bold that they seemed to glow.

Their design won. Over three weekends, volunteers helped them paint the wall. When it was finished, the council planned a small ceremony to unveil it. A large blue tarp hung over the mural until the moment Yusuf and Mei pulled it down, and the crowd saw the painting for the first time.

Now people stop on the sidewalk to look. Some even take pictures with the painted mail carrier, who waves back at everyone who passes.$q$, 'draft')
