-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('aeaf9011-fd97-5b0f-85cc-5efe2f2e41d8', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$How Honeybees Keep Their Hive Comfortable$q$, $q$A honeybee colony can contain tens of thousands of insects, yet the hive stays close to the same temperature for most of the year. The center of the nest, where young bees develop, is usually kept near 35 degrees Celsius. Bees manage this without any machines. They rely on teamwork and a few simple behaviors.

When the hive grows too warm, worker bees gather near the entrance and fan their wings. The moving air pushes hot air out and draws cooler air in. On very hot days, other workers collect drops of water and spread them over the wax combs. As the water evaporates, it lowers the temperature inside.

Cold weather calls for a different plan. In winter, the bees form a tight cluster around their queen. Bees on the inside shiver their flight muscles without moving their wings, which produces heat. Bees on the outer layer pack closely together and act like a blanket. Over time, the bees trade places, so no single bee stays on the chilly outside for too long.

Beekeepers have learned to watch for signs of trouble. A large crowd of bees fanning at the entrance on a mild day may mean the hive is overcrowded. A weak cluster in winter may mean the colony did not store enough honey, since bees burn honey for energy when they shiver.

Scientists who study bees point out that no single bee is in charge of the temperature. Each worker responds to the conditions around her. Together, those small actions keep the whole colony safe.$q$, 'draft')
