   $q$Which detail best supports the inference that a beekeeper can learn about a hive's health just by watching it?$q$, $q$[{"text":"A crowd of bees fanning at the entrance on a mild day may mean overcrowding.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Bees inside the winter cluster shiver their flight muscles to make heat.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"The center of the nest is usually kept near 35 degrees Celsius all year.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Some workers spread drops of water over the wax combs on hot days.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A crowd of bees fanning at the entrance on a mild day may mean overcrowding.$q$, $q$Paragraph 4 explains that a beekeeper who sees bees fanning on a mild day can suspect overcrowding, which is learning about the hive by watching. The temperature detail is true but does not show what a beekeeper observes.$q$, 'draft', 'aeaf9011-fd97-5b0f-85cc-5efe2f2e41d8')
