   $q$Which evidence best supports the inference that electric lights reduced the need for lamplighters?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Residents came to know the lamplighter who worked on their street.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Gas lamps gave off a dim, flickering glow, and leaks could be dangerous.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"},{"text":"Cities began switching electric streetlights on from a central station.","is_correct":true},{"text":"A few towns still keep gas lamps in their historic districts today.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-supporting-detail"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Cities began switching electric streetlights on from a central station.$q$, $q$If one central station turned on the lights, no one had to walk from post to post, so lamplighters were no longer needed. The dim glow and leaks explain why gas had limits, but not why the workers themselves were not needed.$q$, 'draft', '5a0745b1-5ec2-5359-a0f2-84878d4b04ea')
