-- Passage rg7-evidence-inference-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('002f5543-056f-5f8d-bde8-04237c0a3b55', 'rg7-evidence-inference-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-evidence-inference'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Weather Station on the Roof$q$, $q$Last fall, a seventh-grade science club at Fairmont Middle School set out to answer a simple question. Is the weather at our school the same as the weather in the forecast? The nearest official weather station sits at an airport about twenty miles away. The students suspected that conditions on their hilltop campus might be different.

With help from their teacher, Ms. Priya Natarajan, the club built a small weather station from a kit. It included sensors for temperature, humidity, wind speed and rainfall. The sensors connected to a tiny computer that recorded a reading every ten minutes. After getting permission from the principal, the students mounted the station on the roof of the gym.

The first weeks were frustrating. The temperature readings jumped several degrees every afternoon, even on cloudy days. Club member Darnell Brooks noticed that the station sat near a metal air vent that blew warm air from the gym. The students moved the station to the opposite corner of the roof, and the strange jumps stopped.

By spring, the club had collected months of data. They compared their records with the airport's. Their campus was usually two or three degrees cooler. It was also windier, and on several days it recorded rain when the airport recorded none.

The students shared their findings with the school's athletic director. Coaches now check the club's website before outdoor practices, especially on windy days. Next year, the club plans to add a sensor that measures air quality.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
