-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('fcdfaa01-2c15-5899-b5f6-d27183a93eb3', 'rg7-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Garden on Elm Street$q$, $q$When the city gave the neighborhood an empty lot, Mrs. Adeyemi called a meeting on her front porch. Twelve neighbors came, and by the end of the evening they had a plan for a community garden.

The lot was small. Darnell, who was eleven, called it "cramped." His older sister, Tamika, called the same space "cozy." Mrs. Adeyemi just smiled and handed them both shovels.

The work was slow. The soil was packed hard, and during the first week the group pulled out bottle caps, broken bricks, and roots as thick as a wrist. Mr. Halvorsen, who lived next door, refused to stop until every rock was gone. Some neighbors grumbled that he was stubborn. Tamika said he was determined, and she joined him at the end of each day.

By June, the garden had six raised beds bursting with tomatoes, beans, and peppers. Darnell had to admit the lot no longer looked cramped. Every inch was used, and the paths between the beds were just wide enough for a wheelbarrow.

At the first harvest party, Mrs. Adeyemi gave a short speech. She thanked everyone for their hard work and called Mr. Halvorsen "the garden's backbone." He turned pink and stared at his boots. Then the neighbors filled their baskets, and the evening smelled like basil and warm earth.$q$, 'draft')
