   $q$Darnell calls the lot "cramped," and Tamika calls it "cozy." How are these two words different?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Both mean small, but \"cramped\" sounds pleasant and \"cozy\" sounds unpleasant.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"Both words mean exactly the same thing and give the same feeling.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"},{"text":"\"Cramped\" means large and open, while \"cozy\" means small and tight.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"Both mean small, but \"cozy\" sounds pleasant and \"cramped\" sounds uncomfortable.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Both mean small, but "cozy" sounds pleasant and "cramped" sounds uncomfortable.$q$, $q$The narrator says they describe "the same space," so both words mean small, but "cozy" suggests comfort and "cramped" suggests being squeezed. Saying the words feel the same ignores their different connotations.$q$, 'draft', 'fcdfaa01-2c15-5899-b5f6-d27183a93eb3')
