-- Passage rg7-figurative-language-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('dd2590a3-c0e6-5a34-89a6-fa7468410075', 'rg7-figurative-language-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-figurative-language'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Weak Spot in a Strong Bridge$q$, $q$A suspension bridge looks delicate from far away. Its long roadway seems to hang in the air, held up by cables as thin as thread. Up close, however, each main cable is made of thousands of steel wires bundled together, and it can hold millions of pounds.

Engineers often describe these bridges with words that suggest grace, such as "slender" and "elegant." The same bridge could be called "skinny" or "flimsy," but those words would give readers a very different and false impression. A well-built suspension bridge is light for its size, yet it is remarkably strong.

Strength alone is not enough, though. Every bridge has an Achilles' heel, a single weak point that could cause trouble if it is ignored. For many older bridges, that weak point is rust. Water seeps into tiny gaps in the cable wrapping, and over many years it slowly eats away at the steel.

To fight this hidden enemy, inspectors climb the cables and use special sensors to check for damage. Some bridges now have machines that blow dry air through the cables, like a giant hair dryer that never shuts off. This keeps moisture away from the wires.

Taking care of a bridge is a marathon, not a sprint. The work never really ends, because wind, salt, and traffic wear on the structure every day. When inspectors and engineers do their jobs well, drivers cross safely without ever thinking about the steady effort beneath their tires.$q$, 'draft')
