   $q$The author says calling a bridge "flimsy" instead of "slender" would give "a very different and false impression." Why?$q$, $q$[{"text":"\"Flimsy\" is a kinder word that makes the bridge sound graceful.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"reversed-connotation"},{"text":"\"Flimsy\" suggests weak and easily broken, but the bridge is strong.","is_correct":true},{"text":"\"Flimsy\" means the bridge is wide, but the bridge is actually thin.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-denotation"},{"text":"\"Flimsy\" and \"slender\" mean the same thing and feel the same way.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignores-connotation"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$"Flimsy" suggests weak and easily broken, but the bridge is strong.$q$, $q$Both words can describe something thin, but "flimsy" has a negative feeling of weakness, and the author says the bridge "is remarkably strong." "Slender" is the graceful word, so saying "flimsy" is kinder reverses the connotations.$q$, 'draft', 'dd2590a3-c0e6-5a34-89a6-fa7468410075')
