   $q$In paragraph 3, water "slowly eats away at the steel." What does this figurative phrase mean?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The water quickly washes the steel cables clean.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"wrong-connotation"},{"text":"The water gradually damages and wears down the metal.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Small animals in the water chew holes in the cables.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"literal-reading"},{"text":"The water makes the steel heavier and stronger over time.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"opposite-meaning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The water gradually damages and wears down the metal.$q$, $q$"Eats away" compares rust to something slowly consuming the steel "over many years," which means gradual damage. Nothing in the passage mentions animals; the phrase is not literal.$q$, 'draft', 'dd2590a3-c0e6-5a34-89a6-fa7468410075')
