-- Passage rg7-literary-structure-pov-p01
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('09c2cecc-2d16-5b5d-a6f0-982e0ae381ca', 'rg7-literary-structure-pov-p01', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Volcano Table$q$, $q$CHARACTERS: PRIYA, a seventh grader. DESHAWN, her project partner. MS. LINDQVIST, a science fair judge.

(A school gym lined with tables. PRIYA and DESHAWN stand behind a cardboard model of a volcano. Under the table, hidden from the students' view, a plastic bottle has tipped over and is slowly leaking red liquid onto the floor.)

PRIYA: (whispering) Is everything ready?

DESHAWN: (smiling wide) Everything is perfect. We tested it four times this morning.

(MS. LINDQVIST approaches with a clipboard.)

MS. LINDQVIST: Good afternoon. Tell me about your project.

PRIYA: Our volcano shows how gas pressure builds up inside the earth. When we add the baking soda, the pressure pushes the lava out the top.

MS. LINDQVIST: And where is the lava now?

DESHAWN: (proudly patting the volcano) Safe inside, waiting for its big moment.

(The red puddle under the table spreads toward the judge's shoes. Neither student looks down.)

MS. LINDQVIST: Very well. Show me the eruption.

(PRIYA pours the baking soda into the top. Nothing happens. She pours more. Still nothing.)

PRIYA: (nervously) Sometimes the earth needs a minute.

MS. LINDQVIST: (glancing at the floor, then back at the students) It seems your volcano has found another way out.

(DESHAWN looks down at the puddle. His mouth falls open.)

DESHAWN: Well, that is actually very realistic. Real lava finds cracks in the rock.

MS. LINDQVIST: (writing on her clipboard, trying not to smile) Explain that clearly, and you might just save your score.$q$, 'draft')
