-- Passage rg7-literary-structure-pov-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1b91cd7e-99cc-59ac-b6e9-c992cac94d2d', 'rg7-literary-structure-pov-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-literary-structure-pov'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Last Five Minutes$q$, $q$CHARACTERS: LENA, team captain. RAFAEL, the team's programmer. GRACE, the newest team member.

(A practice room. A small robot sits on a table beside a laptop. A clock on the wall reads 3:55. Entries for the robotics contest close at 4:00.)

LENA: Rafael, does the robot turn left yet?

RAFAEL: (typing fast) Almost. The sensor reads the line, but the robot keeps turning right instead.

LENA: We have five minutes. If it fails, we can't enter.

(GRACE steps forward, then stops. She turns toward the audience.)

GRACE: (aside) I think I see the problem. The sensor is mounted backward, so left looks like right to the robot. But I joined this team two weeks ago. Rafael has been coding since fourth grade. Who am I to tell him?

RAFAEL: (groaning) I've rewritten this part three times!

LENA: (to GRACE) You've been quiet all afternoon. Any ideas?

GRACE: (hesitating) Maybe the sensor? It might be on backward.

(RAFAEL stares at the robot. He flips it over. The sensor is clearly turned around.)

RAFAEL: (laughing) I spent an hour fixing code that was never broken.

(He turns the sensor around. The robot rolls forward and turns perfectly left. The clock reads 3:58.)

LENA: Submit it! Now!

(RAFAEL clicks. Everyone cheers. GRACE turns toward the audience again.)

GRACE: (aside) Next time, I won't wait so long to speak up.$q$, 'draft')
