-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a368281e-8bc7-5fda-9941-eb23355b155e', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Secret Training Tool$q$, $q$Young athletes often think that getting better means practicing harder and longer. Practice matters, but sports scientists have found that rest, especially sleep, is just as important for improving performance.

During deep sleep, the body repairs itself. Hard exercise causes tiny tears in muscle fibers. While an athlete sleeps, the body releases a substance called growth hormone, which helps rebuild those fibers so they come back stronger. Without enough sleep, this repair work is cut short, and muscles stay sore and tired.

Sleep also helps the brain learn new skills. When a basketball player practices a new shot or a swimmer works on a turn, the brain stores information about those movements. Researchers have found that the brain replays and strengthens this information during sleep. In several studies, people who slept after learning a physical task performed it more smoothly the next day than people who stayed awake.

Tired athletes make more mistakes. Lack of sleep slows reaction time, so a goalie may move a split second too late. It also makes it harder to focus and to make quick decisions. Some studies suggest that athletes who sleep too little are more likely to get injured.

Experts recommend that teenagers get eight to ten hours of sleep each night. That can be hard with homework, early school start times and evening games. Coaches suggest a few simple habits: going to bed at the same time each night, putting screens away an hour before bed and keeping the bedroom cool and dark. An athlete who protects sleep is giving the body a chance to turn hard work into real progress.$q$, 'draft')
