-- Passage rg7-main-idea-summary-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('936e37eb-2b76-5e44-b323-e6ee4d0af433', 'rg7-main-idea-summary-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g7-main-idea-summary'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Every Student Should Learn to Cook$q$, $q$Every student learns to read, write and solve equations. Yet many young people finish school without knowing how to cook a simple, healthy meal. Middle schools should require a basic cooking class, because cooking is a life skill that supports health, independence and learning in other subjects.

First, cooking helps students make healthier choices. Many packaged and fast foods contain large amounts of salt, sugar and fat. A student who knows how to make a vegetable stir-fry or a pot of soup has another option. Students who help prepare vegetables are also often more willing to try them.

Second, cooking builds independence. Teenagers will soon be living on their own, managing their own money and time. Knowing how to plan meals, follow a recipe and shop on a budget can save money and prevent stress. These are habits that are easier to build at twelve than at twenty-two.

Cooking also connects to subjects students already study. Doubling a recipe requires working with fractions. Watching bread rise is a lesson in chemistry. Reading a recipe carefully is practice in following written directions. A cooking class can make these ideas feel real instead of abstract.

Some people argue that schools do not have the time or money for cooking classes. This concern is fair, but the class does not need an expensive kitchen. A few hot plates, basic tools and simple recipes are enough to start. A cooking unit could even fit inside an existing health or science course.

Schools exist to prepare students for life. Teaching them how to feed themselves well is one of the most practical ways to do that.$q$, 'draft')
