   $q$Which is the best objective summary of the opinion piece?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The author argues that middle schools should require cooking because it promotes health, independence and learning in math and science. The author also says the class can be done cheaply.","is_correct":true},{"text":"Doubling a recipe uses fractions, and watching bread rise teaches chemistry. Reading a recipe is practice in following directions, so cooking connects to school.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"too-narrow"},{"text":"The author makes an excellent point, and every school in the country should add a cooking class next year. Students will be much happier if they can cook.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"includes-opinion"},{"text":"The author argues that schools should stop teaching reading and equations so that students have more time to learn how to cook healthy meals at home.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The author argues that middle schools should require cooking because it promotes health, independence and learning in math and science. The author also says the class can be done cheaply.$q$, $q$The summary that begins "The author argues that middle schools should require cooking" states the claim, the reasons and the response to cost, without the summarizer's own views. The summary that says "The author makes an excellent point" adds the summarizer's opinion, such as "excellent point," which does not belong in an objective summary.$q$, 'draft', '936e37eb-2b76-5e44-b323-e6ee4d0af433')
